---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/foundational/carbon-cycle-climate">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the foundational lesson on Earth's atmosphere for Environmental Science — the
    unit that introduces the planet's carbon accounts, the greenhouse mechanism, and the two
    other great atmospheric wounds of the industrial age, acid rain and ozone depletion. You
    need no earlier lessons; the carbon cycle is built from a bank-account analogy you
    already understand, and the only arithmetic is multiplication, division and one
    logarithm — explained where it appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the four great carbon reservoirs and the fluxes between them, and say which are fast and which are slow;</li>
    <li>read the Keeling curve, and run the emission-ledger arithmetic that converts emissions into a yearly parts-per-million rise;</li>
    <li>explain the greenhouse effect as a mechanism — what absorbs what, and why a trace gas matters;</li>
    <li>give the three fingerprint lines of evidence that the added CO₂ is human-caused, and name the major feedbacks;</li>
    <li>recount the acid rain and ozone-depletion stories — mechanism, damage and the policy fixes that worked;</li>
    <li>locate each atmospheric story on the right floor of the atmosphere, from the troposphere to the Kármán line.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the page: treat the atmosphere as a set of ledgers. Carbon moves
    between accounts; emissions are a line item; warming is what the balance buys. The
    vocabulary of stocks and flows will carry through every later climate lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>Carbon: the skeleton of life, the currency of climate</h2>
  <p>
    Every living thing is built on carbon chains, and the planet trades carbon between four
    great reservoirs the way a bank moves money between accounts. The <strong>atmosphere</strong>
    holds about 880 billion tonnes of carbon (the unit is written GtC); the <strong>oceans</strong>
    hold roughly 38,000 GtC — the giant account; <strong>soils</strong> hold about 1,600 GtC;
    and <strong>living vegetation</strong> about 550 GtC. The movements between accounts — the
    <em>fluxes</em> — are enormous and, critically, balanced: photosynthesis pulls roughly
    120 GtC out of the air every year, and respiration, decay and fire push almost exactly
    the same amount back. For ten thousand years the books balanced at about 280 parts per
    million (ppm) of atmospheric CO₂.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rank the four accounts by balance" widget="order" config={cccReservoirOrder} />
  <p>
    Since about 1850, humans have been adding a bonus line to the ledger: burning fossil
    carbon — coal, oil, gas — that plants buried over hundreds of millions of years, at a
    rate of roughly 10 GtC per year, about 37 billion tonnes of CO₂. Geology suddenly became
    an annual line item. What that line does to the balance, and what the balance does to
    the temperature, is the rest of this page.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the greenhouse effect is bad, and CO₂ is simply pollution.
    It seems reasonable — the news only ever mentions greenhouse gases in headlines about
    crisis. But the natural greenhouse effect is the reason Earth is habitable: without it,
    the average surface temperature would be about −18 °C instead of +15 °C. CO₂ keeps the
    planet 33 degrees warmer than it would otherwise be — the argument is strictly about the
    <em>extra</em> blanket we are adding, not about the blanket itself. The same distinction
    holds for ozone, as we will see: good up high, bad nearby.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="true or false — the blanket itself" widget="judge" config={cccBlanketJudge} />

  <h2>Reading the curve</h2>
  <p>
    In 1958, Charles David Keeling started measuring CO₂ on the slopes of Mauna Loa in
    Hawaii, and got 315 ppm. The line has risen every year since and now passes 420 ppm —
    the highest in at least 800,000 years of ice-core record. No other dataset in
    environmental science is as famous, or as blunt: it is the single sharpest turn in the
    long record of Earth's carbon.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="put today's CO₂ on the ppm scale" widget="place" config={cccKeelingPlace} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Keeling curve — direct CO₂ measurement at Mauna Loa since 1958; the sharpest turn in the record" widget="plotter" config={keelingCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the rise is not just upward — it is <em>accelerating</em>:
    the curve took about forty years to climb 60 ppm (1958 to the late 1990s) and only about
    twenty to climb the next 60. If the line were flattening, the emission problem would be
    easing; instead each decade steeper than the last shows the world still adding carbon
    faster, not slower. This curve is what every climate number below fights against.
  </p>

  <h2>The four ledgers of the carbon cycle</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>vegetation ledger</strong> is the fast one: forests and plankton draw down
    carbon each growing season and exhale it each decay season — a heartbeat of months. The
    <strong>soil ledger</strong> is the slow one and easy to underestimate: dead roots and
    leaf litter hold about three times the carbon of all living plants, and draining
    peatlands or ploughing prairie releases it over decades. The <strong>ocean ledger</strong>
    is the colossus — CO₂ dissolves directly at the surface (cold water dissolves more,
    which is why warming <em>weakens</em> this sink) and is exported to depth by sinking
    organic matter and cold, dense polar water. It has quietly absorbed roughly a quarter of
    all human emissions, at a price: dissolved CO₂ forms carbonic acid, and surface-ocean pH
    has slipped from about 8.2 to 8.1 — a 30 per cent jump in acidity that stresses
    shell-building life.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each ledger with its character" widget="match" config={cccLedgerMatch} />
  <p>
    And beneath the fast cycle runs the <strong>geological cycle</strong>: volcanism exhales
    carbon from the deep Earth at only about 0.1–0.2 GtC per year, while rock weathering and
    the burial of marine shells and plankton pull carbon down just as slowly. That trickle
    matters because it is the original climate thermostat — over millions of years it
    compensated for a Sun 4 per cent fainter in Earth's youth and set the ancient CO₂ levels
    — but it is a thermostat with geological hands: it cannot respond to a century-scale
    jolt, which is precisely what fossil burning delivered.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> four accounts, huge balanced flows, and one unbalanced line item
    — fossil carbon. Next: the arithmetic that turns a year of emissions into the ppm rise
    on the Keeling curve, and then the mechanism by which that rise warms the planet.
  </p>

  <h2>The arithmetic of the ledger</h2>
  <p>
    The mathematics of the curve is worth learning, because it recurs everywhere in climate
    science. Each ppm of atmospheric CO₂ equals about 2.13 billion tonnes of carbon; only
    about <em>half</em> of what we emit stays airborne — oceans and land absorb the rest —
    so the yearly rise equals emissions times the airborne fraction, divided by 2.13. Run
    the numbers below yourself: at 10 GtC per year emitted and a 45 per cent airborne
    fraction, the air gains about 2.1 ppm each year, and the world can add 100 ppm in under
    half a century at that pace. This little panel is, underneath the diplomacy of every
    international climate negotiation, exactly what the two sides are arguing about.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the pace — spending 100 ppm" widget="estimate" config={cccPpmEstimate} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="the emission ledger — emissions × airborne fraction, in the units that matter" widget="sliderLab" config={fluxCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the rise responds <em>linearly</em> to both sliders —
    halve the emissions or halve the airborne fraction and the yearly rise halves. That
    proportionality is the quiet good news of climate policy: the accounting is honest, so
    avoided emissions are avoided ppm, with no hidden penalty. Notice also what the
    airborne-fraction slider represents: the sinks. The ocean and the land are doing
    humanity the enormous favour of absorbing more than half the emissions, for free — and,
    as the ocean ledger above showed, warming weakens that favour.
  </p>

  <h2>The greenhouse effect: the mechanism</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the air itself: 78 per cent nitrogen, 21 per cent oxygen, 0.9 per cent argon —
    and only about 0.042 per cent CO₂. How can a trace gas matter at all? Because of what it
    <em>does</em> with light. The Sun's energy arrives mostly as visible light, which
    nitrogen, oxygen and CO₂ all ignore; it warms the surface, and the surface re-radiates
    that energy as <strong>infrared</strong>. Here nitrogen and oxygen stay passive, but
    CO₂, water vapour and methane absorb IR strongly — their molecules flex and vibrate at
    exactly those wavelengths — and re-emit the energy in all directions, including back
    down. More of these gases means a thicker optical blanket. The physics was measured
    before the industrial revolution finished: Eunice Foote (1856) and John Tyndall (1859)
    demonstrated CO₂'s heat-trapping in the laboratory, and Svante Arrhenius calculated in
    1896 that doubling CO₂ would warm the planet by several degrees.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the blanks — the mechanism in two moves" widget="cloze" config={cccIrcloze} />
  <p>
    The modern numbers: the CO₂ rise since pre-industrial times (280 to more than 420 ppm —
    not yet a doubling) has already pushed global average surface temperature roughly
    1.2–1.5 °C above the late-1800s baseline, with 2024 the hottest calendar year on record.
    The 2015 Paris Agreement set holding warming "well below 2 °C", aiming for 1.5 °C — the
    Keeling curve above is what those numbers fight against.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why are scientists confident the added CO₂ is <em>ours</em>? Three fingerprints. Fossil
    carbon is ancient, so it is depleted of carbon-13 and contains no carbon-14 — and
    atmospheric ratios of both isotopes are shifting exactly as burning old carbon predicts
    (the "Suess effect"). Atmospheric oxygen is declining in lock-step with the CO₂ rise, as
    combustion consumes it. And ice cores show 800,000 years in which CO₂ never left the
    180–300 ppm band — until the last two centuries.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the faulty line — the fingerprint argument" widget="spot-error" config={cccFingerprintSpot} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a cold winter, a blizzard or one cool summer means
    climate change has paused. It feels reasonable — weather is what we personally
    experience. But weather is the daily roll of the dice; climate is the shape of the dice
    measured over decades. A single cold snap at one place says no more about the global
    trend than one losing streak says about a casino's margin — and the global trend is
    measured as a multi-decade average of the whole planet, which has risen through every
    cold winter on record. Short-term variability was always going to continue; it now
    continues on a rising baseline.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system also answers back. Warmer air holds more water vapour — itself a greenhouse
    gas — which amplifies warming; melting ice replaces white, reflective surface with dark,
    absorptive ocean (the <em>ice–albedo feedback</em>); thawing permafrost releases
    methane; warmer oceans absorb <em>less</em> CO₂. Feedbacks, not linearity, are why the
    problem deserves the whole field of environmental science — and why the response of
    clouds, the hardest feedback to model, remains the largest single uncertainty.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="file the feedbacks" widget="sort-buckets" config={cccFeedbackBuckets} />

  <h2>Acid rain: the wound we healed</h2>
  <p>
    Climate change is not the only way combustion rewrote the sky. <strong>Acid rain</strong>
    comes from sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides released by coal burning and vehicle
    engines; in the atmosphere they oxidise into sulphuric and nitric acid and fall with the
    rain. Natural rain is already mildly acidic (pH ≈ 5.6, from dissolved CO₂), but polluted
    precipitation reached pH 4 and below — ten to a hundred times more acidic — acidifying
    lakes (killing fish eggs), damaging forests, and dissolving limestone buildings and
    statues at visible rates. The fixes were engineering: flue-gas scrubbers on power
    plants, catalytic converters on cars, and the 1990 US Clean Air Act Amendments, which
    capped SO₂ emissions with a tradable-permit market. Acid rain is now the environmental
    movement's clearest success story: emissions fell over 80 per cent, lake chemistry is
    recovering, and the episode proved that global-scale air problems are solvable when the
    mechanism is clear and the measurement honest.
  </p>

  <h2>Ozone depletion: the hole we closed</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ozone depletion</strong> is a different mechanism with a happier ending — so
    far. Stratospheric ozone (O₃) is not pollution but the planet's sunscreen, absorbing the
    UV-B that causes skin cancer and cataracts. In the 1970s chemists — Molina and Rowland,
    later Nobel laureates — predicted that chlorofluorocarbons, the seemingly miraculous
    inert refrigerants, would survive to the stratosphere where UV would crack them open and
    release chlorine; a single chlorine atom then catalytically destroys up to about
    100,000 ozone molecules before retiring. Ground-based measurements found the proof in
    1985: springtime ozone over Antarctica down by a third, the "hole" driven by
    polar-stratospheric clouds that turbocharge chlorine chemistry. The 1987 Montreal
    Protocol phased CFCs out worldwide — the most successful environmental treaty ever
    signed — and the hole is slowly healing, with recovery expected around mid-century.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "ozone is pollution, so the ozone hole is smog-related —
    and how can the hole be bad if ozone itself is poisonous?" The confusion is almost
    universal, and the resolution is altitude. Stratospheric ozone (15–35 km up) shields
    life from UV; ground-level ozone (a component of smog, made from traffic emissions
    reacting in sunlight) damages lungs and crops. Same molecule, opposite verdicts,
    different layers — "good up high, bad nearby" is the exam-proof summary.
  </p>

  <h2>The atmosphere in layers: where each story happens</h2>
  <p>
    The atmosphere is not a uniform blanket but a stack of layers with different jobs, and
    each problem in this chapter lives on its own floor. The <strong>troposphere</strong>
    (surface to about 12 km — thinner at the poles, deeper at the equator) holds 75–80 per
    cent of the air's mass and essentially all its water: weather happens here, and so does
    the greenhouse effect's blanket — the CO₂, water vapour and methane are concentrated
    low — and temperature falls with altitude at about 6.5 °C per km, which is why
    mountaintops are cold. Above it the <strong>stratosphere</strong> runs warmer with
    height because its ozone absorbs UV directly; this is the ozone layer's home (peak
    concentration around 20–25 km), and the reason ozone depletion and acid rain and climate
    change are three different stories with three different addresses: acid rain is a
    <em>tropospheric</em> washout problem, ozone a <em>stratospheric</em> photochemistry
    problem, climate change a whole-column radiation problem. Above that, the mesosphere
    burns up meteors, and the thermosphere hosts the aurora and the space station; by 100 km
    (the Kármán line) the air is too thin for wings. One number to keep: 90 per cent of the
    atmosphere lies below about 16 km — a layer thinner, on the scale of the planet, than
    the skin of an apple.
  </p>

  <h2>The ledger at personal and planetary scale</h2>
  <p>
    The emission arithmetic scales down to you, and the numbers are worth having. A typical
    person in a wealthy economy emits on the order of 10 tonnes of CO₂-equivalent a year
    (including imports and shared infrastructure); the global average is around 6; much of
    sub-Saharan Africa sits under 1. The largest personal lines are usually heating, driving
    and flying — one round-trip transatlantic economy flight is roughly a tonne of CO₂ per
    passenger, about a tenth of an average year spent in a single afternoon — followed by
    food (meat-heavy diets roughly double the food line) and everything wearing the embedded
    emissions of imports. Zoom back out and the distribution is the ethical headline: the
    wealthiest tenth of humanity is responsible for close to half of all emissions, while
    the poorest half contribute barely a tenth — and, as a measure of the imbalance, the
    entire African continent emits less carbon than the United States, Russia or Japan,
    while some African regions are already warming at more than twice the global rate. Every
    climate policy is, underneath, an argument about which of these lines to move first and
    who pays for moving it.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the four great carbon reservoirs, largest first — and say which one is smallest yet matters most to the climate of this century.</li>
    <li>The world emits 10 GtC in a year and about half stays airborne. What is the approximate rise in atmospheric CO₂ that year, in ppm?</li>
    <li>Nitrogen and oxygen make up 99 per cent of the air. Why do they not warm the planet, and why does CO₂ — a fraction of a per cent — do so?</li>
    <li>Give two of the three fingerprint lines of evidence that the added CO₂ comes from burning fossil carbon.</li>
    <li>Stratospheric ozone fell by a third over Antarctica. Why did one treaty fix that problem faster than any climate treaty has moved — and what is the ground-level ozone confusion?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Oceans (about 38,000 GtC), soils (about 1,600), atmosphere
    (about 880), vegetation (about 550). The smallest, the atmosphere, matters most because
    it is the account every flux touches and the one whose balance sets the temperature — a
    few hundred billion tonnes moved quickly is the whole story of this century. (2) About
    5 GtC stays up; 5 ÷ 2.13 ≈ 2.3 ppm — roughly the observed annual rise. (3) Nitrogen and
    oxygen are symmetrical two-atom molecules: visible light passes them, and they have no
    vibrational modes that absorb infrared. CO₂ has three atoms that flex and bend at
    infrared wavelengths, so it intercepts the energy the surface re-radiates and re-emits it
    downward — a thicker blanket from a trace gas. (4) Any two of: the carbon-13 depletion
    and carbon-14 absence of the atmosphere shifting exactly as fossil burning predicts (the
    Suess effect); atmospheric oxygen declining in step with the CO₂ rise (combustion
    consumes O₂); the 800,000-year ice-core record bounded at 180–300 ppm until the last two
    centuries. (5) The chemistry of CFCs was understood, substitutes existed and the cost of
    replacing them was modest, so the Montreal Protocol could phase them out worldwide — a
    narrower problem than energy. The ground-level confusion: ozone protects in the
    stratosphere but is a harmful component of smog at street level — good up high, bad
    nearby.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Carbon trades between four reservoirs — oceans (about 38,000 GtC), soils (1,600), atmosphere (880), vegetation (550) — with fast balanced fluxes that held CO₂ near 280 ppm for ten thousand years.</li>
    <li>Fossil burning adds roughly 10 GtC per year; about half stays airborne, and 1 ppm of CO₂ equals about 2.13 GtC — the ledger arithmetic behind every climate negotiation.</li>
    <li>The Keeling curve (1958: 315 ppm; now past 420) is accelerating — the sharpest turn in an 800,000-year ice-core record bounded at 180–300 ppm.</li>
    <li>The greenhouse mechanism: the surface re-radiates as infrared; CO₂, water vapour and methane absorb it and re-emit downward; the natural effect is worth 33 °C, and the dispute is only about the added thickness.</li>
    <li>The human fingerprint shows in isotopes (Suess effect), declining oxygen and the ice cores; feedbacks — water vapour, ice–albedo, permafrost, weaker sinks — amplify the warming.</li>
    <li>Acid rain: SO₂ and NO<sub>x</sub> became sulphuric and nitric acid; scrubbers, catalytic converters and the 1990 Clean Air Act Amendments cut emissions over 80 per cent — the solvable-problem proof.</li>
    <li>Ozone depletion: CFCs released chlorine that destroys ozone catalytically; the Montreal Protocol (1987) is healing the hole by about mid-century — good up high, bad nearby.</li>
    <li>The atmosphere is layered: weather and the greenhouse blanket in the troposphere, the ozone layer in the stratosphere, and 90 per cent of all air below 16 km.</li>
    <li>Emissions are deeply unequal: the wealthiest tenth cause close to half, the poorest half barely a tenth — the equity question underneath every policy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This page set the stage; the unit now turns to the human layers. Next lessons cover air
    quality and aerosols (the pollution you breathe, and the particles that cool as well as
    choke), then climate modelling and scenarios (how the ledger arithmetic becomes
    projection), environmental economics and policy (who pays, and how), tipping points, and
    finally mitigation and adaptation design. The ledgers taught here — stocks, flows,
    balances — are the grammar every one of those lessons writes in.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The core quantitative question in climate science is <strong>sensitivity</strong>: how
    much does Earth warm per doubling of CO₂ once all feedbacks play out? Charney's 1979
    estimate — "near 3 °C" — has survived four decades of scrutiny; the IPCC AR6 range is
    2.5–4 °C, best estimate 3 °C. That number converts straight into the <em>carbon
    budget</em>: humanity can emit only a finite tonnage for a given temperature goal, and
    at 10-plus GtC per year the remaining budget for 1.5 °C is spent within decades. This is
    why the ledger widget above is more than arithmetic — every international negotiation
    is, underneath the diplomacy, two sides arguing about that panel's inputs.
  </p>
  <p>
    The impacts, meanwhile, arrive through water first. Climate change is already rewriting
    the water cycle: snowpack becomes rain, snowmelt runs earlier and winter flows swell in
    mountain regions, and heavier downpours raise flash-flood risk while infiltrating less
    into aquifers. The stakes concentrate where people and ice meet — Peruvian glaciers have
    lost more than 20 per cent of their mass in 35 years, trimming runoff by about 12 per
    cent to a coastal region where 60 per cent of the country's population lives — and where
    rivers and cities meet: water managers project that scheduled deliveries from a drier
    Colorado River basin could miss their targets the majority of the time as this century
    warms. Evapotranspiration rises almost everywhere, tightening the strain on soils and
    farms even where rainfall barely changes. The carbon ledger and the water ledger are the
    same story told in different units.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deeper questions are where research lives. <strong>Tipping elements</strong> — the
    Atlantic circulation (AMOC), the West Antarctic ice sheet, permafrost carbon — may have
    thresholds past which change becomes self-sustaining and effectively irreversible on
    human timescales; their locations are unknown. <strong>Negative emissions</strong> now
    appear in most 1.5 °C pathways at gigatonne scale — capturing carbon at industrial
    sources, burning biomass with capture and storage, or direct-air capture machines — and
    the debate over them is genuinely two-sided: advocates note that removal is the only way
    to retract carbon already emitted, and that rich countries have both the money and the
    historical responsibility; critics answer that land-hungry bioenergy schemes compete with
    farms and forests, that flagship projects (like the multibillion-dollar Kemper plant in
    Mississippi, which abandoned the technology in 2017) have failed on economics, and that
    promised removal could become a licence to keep burning. Natural solutions —
    afforestation, soil-carbon farming, wetland protection — help but cannot reach
    gigatonne scale without encroaching on farmland. And solar geoengineering, scattering
    sunlight with stratospheric aerosols, would cut temperatures within months at the cost
    of masking rather than curing, with governance that does not exist. Carbon moved
    civilisation from firewood to the Moon; the last chapter of the carbon cycle — whether
    the ledger is rebalanced in time — is being written now, in units of parts per million.
  </p>

